-- title:
--   Parametrising the p-torsion of the Tate curve
-- statement:
--   The standing context is a complete, nontrivially normed field $K$ with ultrametric absolute value and characteristic zero (algebraically closed from the parametrisation section onwards), elements $q,\zeta,t\in K$ and a natural number $p$; `curve q` is the Tate curve $y^2+xy=x^3+a_4(q)x+a_6(q)$ and `pointX q u`, `pointY q u` are the Tate series attached to a parameter $u$. Two objects are defined. The abbreviation [`WeierstrassCurve.n_torsionGen W n`](../def/TateCurve_TorsionParametrization.html#L17) is `Submodule.torsionBy ℤ W.toAffine.Point n`, the subgroup of affine points killed by $n$. For $q\neq 0$ with $\|q\|<1$, $p$ prime, $\zeta$ a primitive $p$-th root of unity and $t^p=q$, `tateTorsionPoint q ζ t … i j` is the affine point with coordinates $\bigl(X(\zeta^i t^j),Y(\zeta^i t^j)\bigr)$ when $i<p$, $j<p$ and $(i,j)\neq(0,0)$, and the point at infinity otherwise; nonsingularity follows from $\zeta^i t^j\neq 0$ together with `tateParam_offLattice`, which says that $q^n\zeta^i t^j\neq 1$ for all $n\in\mathbb Z$ in that index range. For a prime $p\ge 5$ over algebraically closed $K$, `tateTorsionEquiv` assembles these into a bijection $\mathrm{Fin}\,p\times\mathrm{Fin}\,p\simeq$ `(curve q).n_torsionGen p`, injectivity resting on `tateParam_class_eq` (index pairs in range with $\zeta^{i'}t^{j'}=q^k\zeta^i t^j$ coincide) and surjectivity on `eq_zero_or_eq_tateParam_unconditional`; `natCard_n_torsionGen_curve` records $\#E_q[p]=p^2$.
--
--   The accompanying theorems express additivity of the uniformisation: `point_inv_eq_neg` ($u^{-1}$ gives the negative of the point of $u$), the dichotomies `point_mul_eq_add_or_sub_of_symAddHyps` and `point_mul_eq_add_or_neg_of_symAddHyps_of_diff`, and full additivity in generic position `point_mul_eq_add_of_symAddHyps_of_diffHyp`; the hypotheses are the predicate `SymAddHyps q`, whose fields `sum` and `prod` give the symmetric sum and product identities for $X(uv)$, $X(uv^{-1})$, and `DiffHyp q`, the identity $\bigl(X(uv)-X(uv^{-1})\bigr)\bigl(X(u)-X(v)\bigr)^2=-\psi(u)\psi(v)$ with $\psi(u)=2Y(u)+X(u)$, for all admissible pairs. From these, `muTranslation_of_symAddHyps_of_diffHyp` and `muTranslation_unconditional` establish `MuTranslation q ζ p`: for $1\le a<p$ and $u$ with $u$ and $\zeta^a u$ off the lattice, the point of $\zeta^a u$ is the sum of the points of $\zeta^a$ and of $u$. `zeta_pow_point_eq_nsmul` and `tateTorsionPoint_fst_eq_nsmul` identify the point of $\zeta^i$ with $i$ times the point of $\zeta$, and `map_pointX_tateParam`, `map_pointY_tateParam` show that a ring isometry $\sigma$ of $K$ with $\sigma q=q$, $\sigma\zeta=\zeta^e$, $\sigma t=\zeta^c t$ carries the coordinates of the pair $(i,j)$ to those of $(ei+cj,\,j)$, the line $j=0$ being preserved (`map_pointX_tateParam_toric`).
--
--   **Relation to Mathlib.** `n_torsionGen` is an abbreviation for Mathlib's `Submodule.torsionBy ℤ` applied to Mathlib's group `WeierstrassCurve.Affine.Point`, whose addition law and nonsingularity predicate are used throughout; Mathlib has no Tate curve or Tate uniformisation, so the Tate series, the parametrisation and the equivalence are the project's own. The lemma `addX_sub_addX_negY_mul_sq`, placed in the `WeierstrassCurve.Affine` namespace, is the antisymmetric companion of the sum and product identities for the two `addX` values.
--
--   **Where it is used.** These results give the multiplicative description of $E_q[p]$ for a curve with multiplicative reduction: the $p$-torsion has exactly $p^2$ points, parametrised by $\zeta^i t^j$, and the subgroup generated by the point of $\zeta$ is stable under isometric automorphisms fixing $q$. This is what makes the mod-$p$ representation attached to a curve with multiplicative reduction upper-triangular on inertia at such a prime, as needed in the analysis of the Frey curve.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Definitions/Def_TateCurve_TorsionParametrization.lean

